<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item1> a pr:ChoiceItem,
        pr:Item ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Synthesis ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:optionSetKey "thecoinswenttoanaddresswithnokeybehinditcheckingtheaddressagainwouldhavecaughtit|themissingbytechangedtheprivatekeyonlyasecondcopyofthepaperwouldhavecaughtit|thewalletlooksonanothernetworkatinyfirstpaymentandadayswaitwouldhavecaughtit|thewallettookthekeyasuncompressedandwatchesitsotheraddresscomparingthefirstaddressafteratestrestorewouldhavecaughtit" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 4 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch04_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_46>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch04_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_61>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch04_quiz_v1_0_0_json_2>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch05_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_113> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP4.09>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP4.10>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP4.13>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP5.07> ;
    pr:bloomLevel 4 ;
    pr:chapter 4,
        5 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i14:wif-flag-and-balance" ;
    pr:itemType pr:ChoiceFour ;
    pr:option <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item1_optA>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item1_optB>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item1_optC>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item1_optD> ;
    pr:position 1 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src1> ;
    pr:stem "Dana's coins were sent to the address of the compressed public key of her private key. Her paper backup holds that key in Wallet Import Format (WIF), the text form for importing a private key; a WIF for a compressed key ends with an extra byte 01, but hers does not. A new wallet imports it and shows zero balance. Tips: compressed public key: the shorter form of a public key; a private key has two public key forms and so two addresses, and coins sent to one are not seen at the other. Which diagnosis fits, and which check before funding would have caught it?" ;
    pr:synthesis true .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item10> a pr:CodeTraceItem,
        pr:Item ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Direct ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 3 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch03_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_29>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch03_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_38> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP3.03> ;
    pr:bloomLevel 3 ;
    pr:chapter 3 ;
    pr:code """tags = ['v28.0', 'v3.1', 'v28.0rc1',
        'v4.0', 'v24.2', 'v9.1']
final = [t for t in tags if 'rc' not in t]
def key(t):
    return [int(p) for p in t[1:].split('.')]
a = sorted(final)
b = sorted(final, key=key)
move = 0
for t in final:
    move += abs(a.index(t) - b.index(t))
print(move)""" ;
    pr:codeLines 11 ;
    pr:codeMaxColumns 45 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i11:trace-release-tags" ;
    pr:itemType pr:CodeTrace ;
    pr:keyOutput "12" ;
    pr:position 10 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src10> ;
    pr:stem "A script drops the release candidates from a list of release tags and compares the final releases sorted as text with the same releases sorted by version number. It adds up how far each tag moves between the two orders. Tips: release tag: the name of one version of a program, such as v3.1; release candidate: a test version before the final one; its tag carries rc; sorted as text: letter by letter, so \"v28.0\" comes before \"v3.1\". What does it print?" .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item11> a pr:Item,
        pr:NumericItem ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Direct ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 4 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch04_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_156>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch04_quiz_v1_0_0_json_6> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP4.12> ;
    pr:answerBoxes 1 ;
    pr:bloomLevel 3 ;
    pr:chapter 4 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i13:vanity-factor" ;
    pr:itemType pr:NumericAnswer ;
    pr:keyValue 3364.0 ;
    pr:position 11 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src11> ;
    pr:stem "A search for a vanity address needs, on average, a number of tries for one chosen letter after the leading 1. Nobody can shorten the search. Tips: vanity address: an address chosen to start with letters its owner likes; 58 symbols: an address is written with 58 symbols, so each further chosen letter makes a match 58 times less likely. By what factor is the average number of tries larger when the search asks for three chosen letters instead of one?" ;
    pr:tolerance 0.0 ;
    pr:unit "times" .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item12> a pr:CodeTraceItem,
        pr:Item ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Direct ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 6 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch06_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_80>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch06_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_81>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch06_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_89> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP6.02>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP6.10> ;
    pr:bloomLevel 4 ;
    pr:chapter 6 ;
    pr:code """seqs = [0xffffffff, 0xfffffffd, 0x0000001e,
        0x80000010, 0x00400005]
total = 0
for s in seqs:
    replace = s < 0xfffffffe
    timed = (s >> 31) == 0
    if replace and timed:
        total += s & 0xffff
print(total)""" ;
    pr:codeLines 9 ;
    pr:codeMaxColumns 43 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i22:trace-sequence-field" ;
    pr:itemType pr:CodeTrace ;
    pr:keyOutput "35" ;
    pr:position 12 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src12> ;
    pr:stem "The program reads five sequence values (a 32-bit number in each input). A value signals opt-in replacement when it is below 0xfffffffe and carries a relative timelock when bit 31 is clear. It adds up the low 16 bits of the values that do both. Tips: opt-in replacement: the unconfirmed payment may be replaced by one with a higher fee; relative timelock: the spent output must first wait a set time; bit 31: the highest of the 32 bits; clear means 0; 0x: starts a hexadecimal number. What does it print?" .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item13> a pr:Item,
        pr:NumericItem ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Direct ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 1 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch01_question_bank_v1_1_0_json_52>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch01_question_bank_v1_1_0_json_53> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP1.02>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP1.11> ;
    pr:answerBoxes 1 ;
    pr:bloomLevel 3 ;
    pr:chapter 1 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i04:vwap-third-trade" ;
    pr:itemType pr:NumericAnswer ;
    pr:keyValue 65000.0 ;
    pr:position 13 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src13> ;
    pr:stem "Trades discover the price of bitcoin. On one day a price service sees three trades of bitcoin: 1 bitcoin at 62,000 dollars, 2 bitcoin at 63,000 dollars, and 1 bitcoin at a price that is not shown. The service publishes a volume-weighted figure for the day of 63,250 dollars per bitcoin. Tips: volume-weighted figure: an average in which each trade counts in proportion to the amount traded, so a large trade moves it more than a small one. At what price, in dollars per bitcoin, did the third trade happen?" ;
    pr:tolerance 0.0 ;
    pr:unit "dollars per bitcoin" .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item14> a pr:CodeTraceItem,
        pr:Item ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Direct ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 6 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch06_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_39> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP6.21> ;
    pr:bloomLevel 3 ;
    pr:chapter 6 ;
    pr:code """values = [252, 253, 65536, 4294967295]
sizes = [len(cs_encode(n)) for n in values]
print(sum(sizes))""" ;
    pr:codeLines 3 ;
    pr:codeMaxColumns 43 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i21:trace-compactsize" ;
    pr:itemType pr:CodeTrace ;
    pr:keyOutput "14" ;
    pr:position 14 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src14> ;
    pr:stem "The function cs_encode writes a count in the CompactSize encoding: a count below 253 takes 1 byte, up to 65535 takes 3 bytes, up to 4294967295 takes 5 bytes, anything larger takes 9 bytes. The program measures four counts that sit at the edges where the encoding changes length. What does it print?" .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item15> a pr:Item,
        pr:NumericItem ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Direct ;
    en:normStem "arecoverycodehas18wordseachwordcarries11bitsandthechecksumisonebitforevery32bitsofentropytipsrecoverycodewordsthatwritedownthesecretawalletisbuiltfromentropytheamountofrandomnessinthesecretinbitschecksumextrabitsthatcatchamistypedwordhowmanybitsofentropydoesthecodehold" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 5 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch05_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_18>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch05_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_20>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch05_quiz_v1_0_0_json_1> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP5.03>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP5.08> ;
    pr:answerBoxes 1 ;
    pr:bloomLevel 3 ;
    pr:chapter 5 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i17:words-to-entropy" ;
    pr:itemType pr:NumericAnswer ;
    pr:keyValue 192.0 ;
    pr:position 15 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src15> ;
    pr:stem "A recovery code has 18 words. Each word carries 11 bits, and the checksum is one bit for every 32 bits of entropy. Tips: recovery code: words that write down the secret a wallet is built from; entropy: the amount of randomness in the secret, in bits; checksum: extra bits that catch a mistyped word. How many bits of entropy does the code hold?" ;
    pr:tolerance 0.0 ;
    pr:unit "bits" .

