        5 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i01:gox-two-events" ;
    pr:itemType pr:ChoiceFour ;
    pr:option <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item4_optA>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item4_optB>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item4_optC>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item4_optD> ;
    pr:position 4 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src4> ;
    pr:stem "Two stories about the exchange Mt. Gox in 2014. On 28 February it filed for bankruptcy after admitting that about 850,000 bitcoin were gone. Three weeks later it said it had found about 200,000 bitcoin in an old-format wallet file it believed was empty. Tips: Mt. Gox: a Tokyo bitcoin exchange; keys: secret numbers that let their holder spend coins; wallet file: stores a wallet's keys; bankruptcy: a court closes a company that cannot pay. Which statement says correctly what each story teaches?" ;
    pr:synthesis true .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item4_optA> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "A" ;
    pr:text "Filing: the network let the exchange spend the same coins twice. Old file: miners held the coins back for a fee." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item4_optB> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "B" ;
    pr:text "Filing: customers lost their own keys. Old file: backups expire, so an old file can be thrown away." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item4_optC> pr:isKey true ;
    pr:letter "C" ;
    pr:text "Filing: whoever holds the keys holds the coins, and customers held none. Old file: an untested file is not an empty one." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item4_optD> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "D" ;
    pr:text "Filing: an untested file is not an empty one. Old file: whoever holds the keys holds the coins." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item5> a pr:ChoiceItem,
        pr:Item ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Synthesis ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:optionSetKey "aleakrevealsnopasswordbecauserpcauthissaltedthep2pkhhashisacommitmentandthekeyappearsonlywhencoinsarespent|aleakrevealsthepasswordbecauserpcauthisonlyanencodingthep2pkhhashcomeswiththekey|bothhashesaresaltedchecksumsthatcatchtyposandbothsecretsappearwhenanodereadsthem|therpcauthhashisacommitmentrevealedwhencoinsarespentthep2pkhhashissaltedsoaleakrevealsnokey" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 3 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch03_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_130>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch03_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_131>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch04_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_79>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch04_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_92> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP3.08>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP4.05> ;
    pr:bloomLevel 4 ;
    pr:chapter 3,
        4 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i12:rpcauth-and-p2pkh" ;
    pr:itemType pr:ChoiceFour ;
    pr:option <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item5_optA>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item5_optB>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item5_optC>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item5_optD> ;
    pr:position 5 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src5> ;
    pr:stem "A node's configuration file holds an rpcauth line, and a pay to public key hash (P2PKH) output script (the lock of the older address format) holds a 20-byte hash of a public key. Both keep a hash, not the secret. Tips: rpcauth: the node setting (remote procedure call authentication) that checks passwords of programs calling the node; hash: a one-way fingerprint; salted: mixed with random data, so equal passwords give different hashes; commitment: fixes a secret now; its owner reveals it later. Which statement compares them correctly, and why do both keep a hash?" ;
    pr:synthesis true .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item5_optA> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "A" ;
    pr:text "A leak reveals the password, because rpcauth is only an encoding; the P2PKH hash comes with the key." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item5_optB> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "B" ;
    pr:text "The rpcauth hash is a commitment revealed when coins are spent; the P2PKH hash is salted, so a leak reveals no key." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item5_optC> pr:isKey true ;
    pr:letter "C" ;
    pr:text "A leak reveals no password, because rpcauth is salted; the P2PKH hash is a commitment, and the key appears only when coins are spent." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item5_optD> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "D" ;
    pr:text "Both hashes are salted checksums that catch typos, and both secrets appear when a node reads them." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item6> a pr:ChoiceItem,
        pr:Item ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Synthesis ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:optionSetKey "nothe21000satoshisleftoveraretheminersfeethedifferencebetweeninputsandoutputsnofieldrecordsit|nothe21000satoshisreturntothewalletaschangesonofeeispaid|yesfouroutputsmakeitaconsolidationwhichmustspendexactlyitsinputs|yestheledgermustbalanceexactlysooneoutputmustbeloweredby21000" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 1 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch02_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_62>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch02_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_64>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch02_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_9> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP1.06>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP2.02>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP2.14> ;
    pr:bloomLevel 3 ;
    pr:chapter 1,
        2 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i02:fee-not-written" ;
    pr:itemType pr:ChoiceFour ;
    pr:option <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item6_optA>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item6_optB>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item6_optC>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item6_optD> ;
    pr:position 6 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src6> ;
    pr:stem "A wallet shows a draft batching payment: inputs of 400,000 and 250,000 satoshis, outputs of 300,000, 200,000, 100,000 and 29,000 satoshis. A colleague says that every node will refuse it because inputs and outputs are not equal. Tips: satoshi: one hundred-millionth of a bitcoin; batching: one transaction paying several people; node: a computer that checks the rules; consolidation: many small coins gathered into one; change: the part of the inputs that returns to the sender. Which reply is correct, and why?" ;
    pr:synthesis true .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item6_optA> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "A" ;
    pr:text "Yes: four outputs make it a consolidation, which must spend exactly its inputs." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item6_optB> pr:isKey true ;
    pr:letter "B" ;
    pr:text "No: the 21,000 satoshis left over are the miner's fee, the difference between inputs and outputs; no field records it." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item6_optC> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "C" ;
    pr:text "No: the 21,000 satoshis return to the wallet as change, so no fee is paid." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item6_optD> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "D" ;
    pr:text "Yes: the ledger must balance exactly, so one output must be lowered by 21,000." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item7> a pr:ChoiceItem,
        pr:Item ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Direct ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:optionSetKey "apassphraseforksthewalletandlabelscannotbederivedfromtheseedshemustenterthepassphraseandimportthelabelsfile|labelscomefromtheseedandwerelostwiththephonethepassphraseonlychangesthespelling|thegaplimitstoppedthescantooearlyalargeronewouldbringbackthebalanceandthelabels|therecoverycodewasonthestolenphonesothethiefhasspentthecoins" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 5 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch05_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_113>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch05_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_142>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch05_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_31> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP5.05>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP5.09>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP5.11>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP5.15>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP5.17> ;
    pr:bloomLevel 4 ;
    pr:chapter 5 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i20:restore-passphrase-labels" ;
    pr:itemType pr:ChoiceFour ;
    pr:option <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item7_optA>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item7_optB>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item7_optC>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item7_optD> ;
    pr:position 7 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src7> ;
    pr:stem "Lena's phone is stolen. From her twelve-word recovery code a new phone shows different addresses, an empty balance and no labels. She had set a passphrase and had exported her labels in the Bitcoin Improvement Proposal 329 (BIP329) format, a standard label file. Tips: recovery code: words that write down the secret a wallet is built from; passphrase: an extra secret; the same words with another passphrase open another wallet; labels: notes on addresses and payments; gap limit: unused keys in a row after which a recovery scan stops. What is the most complete explanation and the fix?" .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item7_optA> pr:isKey true ;
    pr:letter "A" ;
    pr:text "A passphrase forks the wallet and labels cannot be derived from the seed; she must enter the passphrase and import the labels file." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item7_optB> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "B" ;
    pr:text "The recovery code was on the stolen phone, so the thief has spent the coins." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item7_optC> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "C" ;
    pr:text "Labels come from the seed and were lost with the phone; the passphrase only changes the spelling." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item7_optD> pr:isKey false ;
    pr:letter "D" ;
    pr:text "The gap limit stopped the scan too early; a larger one would bring back the balance and the labels." .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item8> a pr:CodeTraceItem,
        pr:Item ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Direct ;
    en:normStem "awalletthatisrecoveredfromitscodescansitskeysinorderandstopsaftergapunusedkeysinarowthewalletreceivedpaymentsonkeys0312and30theprogramaddsupthenumbersofthekeysthatthescanfindswhatdoesitprintused031230gaprunfound100foriinrange40ifiinusedfoundappendirun0elserun1ifrungapbreakprintsumfound" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 5 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch05_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_161> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP5.20> ;
    pr:bloomLevel 4 ;
    pr:chapter 5 ;
    pr:code """used = {0, 3, 12, 30}
gap, run, found = 10, 0, []
for i in range(40):
    if i in used:
        found.append(i)
        run = 0
    else:
        run += 1
        if run == gap:
            break
print(sum(found))""" ;
    pr:codeLines 11 ;
    pr:codeMaxColumns 27 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i19:trace-gap-limit" ;
    pr:itemType pr:CodeTrace ;
    pr:keyOutput "15" ;
    pr:position 8 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src8> ;
    pr:stem "A wallet that is recovered from its code scans its keys in order and stops after gap unused keys in a row. The wallet received payments on keys 0, 3, 12 and 30. The program adds up the numbers of the keys that the scan finds. What does it print?" .

<http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_item9> a pr:Item,
        pr:NumericItem ;
    en:copyChecked true ;
    en:kind en:Synthesis ;
    en:normStem "" ;
    en:origin en:Authored ;
    en:primaryChapter 1 ;
    en:seedItem <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch01_question_bank_v1_1_0_json_4>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch01_quiz_v1_0_0_json_1>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/page#ch06_question_bank_v1_0_0_json_171> ;
    en:tests <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP1.02>,
        <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/criticalpoint#CP6.18> ;
    pr:answerBoxes 1 ;
    pr:bloomLevel 3 ;
    pr:chapter 1,
        6 ;
    pr:itemKey "mt2:i03:toy-chain-supply" ;
    pr:itemType pr:NumericAnswer ;
    pr:keyValue 3900.0 ;
    pr:position 9 ;
    pr:source <http://example.org/sen0401/engine/paper#midterm_v3_src9> ;
    pr:stem "A toy chain pays a block subsidy of 40 coins. Every 50 blocks the subsidy is halved by a right shift, so any fraction is dropped, and once it reaches zero no more coins are created. The same arithmetic limits the real supply to about 21 million. Tips: block subsidy: the new coins that each block creates for its miner; right shift: moving every binary digit one place to the right, which halves a whole number and drops the fraction (for example 5 becomes 2). How many coins does the subsidy create in total over the whole life of the chain?" ;
    pr:synthesis true ;
    pr:tolerance 0.0 ;
    pr:unit "coins" .

